<title>BELGIUM: Belgian banks want state to compensate Euro cost.</title>
<headline>Belgian banks want state to compensate Euro cost.</headline>
<dateline>BRUSSELS 1996-11-28</dateline>
<text>
<p>Belgian banks want the Belgian state to compensate part of the estimated 35 billion franc cost of switching to the European single currency in 1999, a Belgian Banking Association (BBA) spokesman said on Thursday.</p>
<p>&quot;Our question to the government is: Do we have to pay this alone? What form of help we can receive can be discussed, but we do not want to carry that cost alone,&quot; he said, confiming the comments of BBA chairman Fred Chaffart in Belgian papers.</p>
<p>The BBA estimates that converting computer systems to the Euro will cost 15 billion francs, lost income from currency trade 15 billion and the conversion of cash money five billion francs.  </p>
<p>The spokesman said it was hoped that Finance Minister Philippe Maystadt would address the issue when he came to give a speech at the BBA's annual meeting next Wednesday.</p>
